</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide a more formal treatment of the information bottleneck formulation in LaME.
Let <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> denote the input (interleaved text and visual tokens),
<math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> the representation carried by the reason token hidden states <math id="A3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="A3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> the supervision signal from both decoder and embedding heads.
The IB objective is:</p>
<table id="A3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{p(z|x)}\;I(Z;X)-\beta\,I(Z;Y)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.447em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{p(z|x)}\;I(Z;X)-\beta\,I(Z;Y),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> trades off compression against predictive power.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In LaME, the capacity constraint is structural rather than variational:
the representation <math id="A3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is supported on at most <math id="A3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> tokens of dimensionality <math id="A3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>,
imposing a hard information ceiling
<math id="A3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(Z;X)\leq K\cdot d\cdot\log(1+\text{SNR})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mtext>SNR</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(Z;X)\leq K\cdot d\cdot\log(1+\text{SNR})</annotation></semantics></math>,
where SNR denotes the effective signal-to-noise ratio of the hidden states.
This is distinct from variational IB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alemi et al., 2017</a>)</cite>,
which replaces <math id="A3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(Z;X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(Z;X)</annotation></semantics></math> with a KL-divergence upper bound requiring an auxiliary prior and Monte Carlo estimation;
here, the token count <math id="A3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> itself serves as the bottleneck radius.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The dual-head supervision provides the predictive term <math id="A3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(Z;Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(Z;Y)</annotation></semantics></math> via two complementary channels:
the decoder head maximizes <math id="A3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(Z_{r};Y_{\text{dec}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mtext>dec</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(Z_{r};Y_{\text{dec}})</annotation></semantics></math> through autoregressive reconstruction on the first <math id="A3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens,
while the embedding head maximizes <math id="A3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(Z_{e};Y_{\text{emb}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mtext>emb</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(Z_{e};Y_{\text{emb}})</annotation></semantics></math> through contrastive learning on the remaining <math id="A3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens.
Since both heads share the same bottleneck, the finite capacity enforces a joint compression trade-off between reconstructive and discriminative information.
This prevents degeneration into either a reconstructive autoencoder that ignores retrieval structure or a collapsed embedding that discards fine-grained semantics.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The two-stage training can be interpreted as progressively tightening the bottleneck:
Stage 1 (frozen backbone) allows the reason tokens to discover a compact representation without altering the backbone,
while Stage 2 (joint optimization) enables the backbone to co-adapt, routing task-relevant information through the reason tokens while preserving encoding capacity in the [EMBED] token path.
This staged curriculum avoids the trivial solution where the backbone bypasses the bottleneck entirely, which occurs under end-to-end training as shown in Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Results on MMEB-V1. ‣ 4.3 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ LaME: Learning to Think in Latent Space for Multimodal Embedding via Information Bottleneck" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
